[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
This is Orca's server (orcad, what upstream's `orca serve` runs) for
FreeBSD, from upstream's server-only release. The UI runs elsewhere, in
the Orca desktop app on Linux, macOS or Windows. Terminals, agents and
repositories stay on this machine.

Run it as a service:

  sysrc orca_server_enable=YES
  sysrc orca_server_pairing_address=<address the client dials>
  service orca_server start
  service orca_server pair

`service orca_server pair` prints a "Pairing URL". In the desktop app,
open Settings -> Remote Orca Servers -> Add Server and paste it.

Each link pairs one client; run `service orca_server pair` again for the
next. Treat the links like passwords. Paired clients are revoked in
Settings -> Remote Orca Servers -> Shared Server Access.

Or run it in the foreground as yourself, which prints the same link:

  orca-ide serve --pairing-address <address the client dials>

The server listens on TCP port 6768 (orca_server_port) on every
interface (orca_server_bind). Clients need to reach that port. Keep it
on a private network path (LAN, Tailscale, WireGuard or an SSH tunnel)
and do not expose it to the Internet.

The service runs as user "orca" (home %%ORCA_HOME%%). Agents, git and
terminals run with that user's home, credentials and files. To use
your own repositories and agent logins, set orca_server_user to your own
user instead. Install and log in to the agent CLIs (claude, codex, ...)
as that user. Orca's CLI is `orca-ide`, for example
`orca-ide account add --agent claude`. Point it at the service's profile
with ORCA_USER_DATA when it is not ~/.orca.

The profile holds the server's identity and its paired clients, and it
is in the service user's home. After changing orca_server_user, restart
the service and pair the clients again.

Coming from devel/orca-ide: rename the orca_ide_* variables in rc.conf
to orca_server_*. The profile stays where it was, so paired clients
keep working.

`service orca_server stop` stops the server but, as upstream intends,
not its terminal daemon. Terminals and running agents survive restarts.

Not available on FreeBSD:
- the browser client, which upstream ships only with the desktop app
  (devel/orca-ide includes it);
- the listening-ports view of a workspace;
- per-terminal memory and process limits (they use systemd on Linux);
- server-side browser panes (upstream drives them with Electron or a
  Linux-only helper); pages can still open on the client;
- speech to text, and computer use (`orca computer`);
- `orca serve service install` (use the rc script) and self-update
  (use pkg).
EOM
}
]
